WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.640
A smooth slope of signal should give a blur.

2
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.140
Yet the domains have sharp borders.

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.080
Why?

4
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:15.410
Picture a cell whose output simply rises with the signal, the gentle curve of simple

5
00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:16.410
binding.

6
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:22.673
Going from 10 % to 90 percent output then takes an 81-fold change in concentration.

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.627
On an illustrative slope, that change spans about eighteen cells.

8
00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:30.927
Eighteen cells of in-between answers is a blur.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.980
In this simple model, a steeper, switch-like response narrows the blur to one or two

10
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:37.980
cells.

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.120
The cells are all still there.

12
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.520
Fewer of them give in-between answers.

13
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:46.633
The domains get their switch from the factors themselves.

14
00:00:47.873 --> 00:00:50.233
Factors on either side of a border repress each other.

15
00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:53.596
This was tested by electroporation.

16
00:00:54.656 --> 00:00:57.596
DNA is pushed into cells on one side of the tube.

17
00:00:58.456 --> 00:00:59.916
The other side is the control.

18
00:01:00.966 --> 00:01:06.826
James Briscoe and colleagues showed that Pax6 and Nkx2.2 repress each other.

19
00:01:08.616 --> 00:01:11.576
They meet at the border between pMN and p3.

20
00:01:12.566 --> 00:01:16.886
Novitch, Chen and Jessell found the same for Irx3 and Olig2.

21
00:01:19.666 --> 00:01:24.726
Predict. Force Nkx2.2 on in the motor neuron domain.

22
00:01:25.646 --> 00:01:26.726
What happens to those cells?

23
00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:35.626
Motor neurons are suppressed on that side.

24
00:01:36.466 --> 00:01:39.646
The cells make V3 neurons, the fate of the domain below.

25
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.230
One factor in the wrong place moved the line for those cells.

26
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.970
Nothing was done to the Shh source.

27
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.300
Two factors that repress each other can form a switch with two stable states.

28
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:58.343
In simple models, they do so only when the repression is strong and cooperative.

29
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:00.563
A weak loop makes no switch.

30
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:06.103
In a strongly cross-repressive circuit, a cell near a border cannot hold both

31
00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:10.663
factors for long. It falls to one side, and the loop helps hold it there.

32
00:02:11.616 --> 00:02:13.436
That gives the network some memory.

33
00:02:14.336 --> 00:02:18.456
In 2012, Briscoe's group tested it in mouse embryos in culture.

34
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.400
A short pulse of a drug that activates Smoothened left the borders close to normal.

35
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.880
Continuous treatment shifted them.

36
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:28.980
There is a limit here.

37
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.300
Sharp is not the same as accurate.

38
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.990
A large or lasting misreading can tip a cell to the wrong side.

39
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.310
The switch may then hold the error.

40
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.250
Placing borders accurately is a separate problem.

41
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.010
In models, reading over time, and reading two opposing slopes, can help.

42
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.700
The takeaway: the slope sets roughly where each line falls.

43
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.660
Pairs of repressors make each line sharp, and help hold it.
